                                      THE BRAID




She took me down to the river at sunset. She insisted.
   The wall hadn’t changed. Old stone above brown water that moved
with heavy purpose, a cloud of swifts overhead, a vendor behind us
selling cold coconuts out of a cart. I took out my phone.
   “Llámala,” Abuela said.
   I called my mother.
   She picked up on the first ring, as though she’d been sitting on the
phone for three days, which she had.
   “¿Estás en el río?”
   “Estoy en el río.”
   I held the phone out over the water. I don’t know what it picked up:
the current, the swifts, a coconut vendor’s machete. I kept it there.
When I brought it back, my mother wasn’t speaking. I could hear her
breathing in a kitchen in the Bronx with her hand over her mouth.
   “Má. Abuela wants—”
   Abuela took the phone.
   She held it with both hands, like a thing that might spill. She didn’t
speak for a moment.
   “Altagracia,” she said.
   I couldn’t hear the answer. I saw Abuela’s face receive it.
   “Hiciste bien,” said my grandmother.
   You did right.
   I walked a few steps down the wall and looked at the water and
gave them what privacy a bridge allows. When I came back, the phone
was in my hand again and the line was closed and my grandmother
was looking out at the river with the same expression she’d worn at the
gagá, her mouth shut tight, her chin lifted.
   My phone buzzed.

